s pathogen  it is motol okay motile meaning it can  move  and this particular stage is called a  zospore  so aquatic motor zospores disperse  actually they've been given off by azo  sporangium we'll get to that at the end  and colonize host skin  this right here is pointing to  a flagellum so that is what makes them  modal they are able to swim with the  movement of a flagellum  then the zoospores once they find  some host skin to land on this would be  amphibian skin  the zospores will insist  so insisting means they resorb the  flagellum  and develop a foul wall  so the zoospores they um  resorb their flagella they develop a  cell wall that's called insisting or  insistent  and develop into the next life stage  which is called a phallus  now the plural of phthalates is phthali  so multiple of that phthalates life  stage would be called stalais  and they have  these  rhizoid  these structures called rhizoids so  these are kind of filamentous appendages  coming off that kind of help with like  attachment and even growing these in  culture when there is no host skin for  them to attach to you can still see  these rhizoids under a microscope  so then eventually the phthalates and  the phthalus is a little bit bigger than  the zospore the zospores are like two to  three microns and the phallus might be  like  five or six microns i'm trying to  remember that off the top of my head the  stylus will mature into an even larger  structure called a zosporangium  okay so matures into a zosporantium and  this is where new  zospores are formed so you see that  there is a cap  right there and so that cap it's like a  plug right it um seals that discharge  tube  and you've got the zoos force forming  inside what happens is you basically  have a lot of  a lot of dna synthesis in here you kind  of have something that looks like  multi-nucleated and then there are these  cleavage events that separate  those into the individual zoo spores  and then the kind of final stage of this  process is that once a cat dissolves  zoospores are released through one or  more  what are called discharge  papillae  or sometimes we just say discharge tubes  but the papillae  are the discharge tubes now i've drawn  ones here that have one  of those discharge tubes they look kind  of like urns but certainly when i was  studying this fungus i'd look at it  under a microscope  and i would regularly see  i'm gonna try to  kind of  draw this i would regularly see  something that looks like this right  full of zo spores it's also cool  because  um you know i could even sometimes see  again when using a microscope i could  see the zo spores you know when they're  at this stage where they're all in there  like actually starting to like wiggle  and move and and and move up through a  recently opened tube  um i've even seen them before with like  three discharge papillae so that can  happen as well  also not shown here but these things um  particularly when growing in culture can  form very large colonial structures  meaning a colony where you can have you  know ten  fifty a hundred of these  zosforangia remember zoe sporangium is  singular zosparangia is plural but you  could have very large numbers of them  all kind of clumps together  um rhizoids entangled  um  into these very complex kind of colonies  now  in red here i've drawn the host skin  surface because i want you to remember  that all of this is happening within the  context of amphibians skin  so the zoospores swim towards the skin  infect the cells insist and then um over  the course of a few days are going  through this process  and as the amphibian skin is shed  these zosporangia are going to get  closer and closer to the surface of the  skin  and their goal  really is for these papillae to be able  to just kind of protrude right out of  the epithelial cell at the surface of  the host's skin  so that these zoo spores aren't  necessarily being released like within  the epithelium of the current host but  are 